BandC wrote:
>
> When playing Nethack, I usually develop my main weapon(s) (the one(s) I
> start with) to Expert -- for roleplaying reasons: I think a character
> should be proficient with his/her main weapon.

Role playing is a valid reason.  It brings up why folks play NetHack
in the first place.  Is the goal ascending or is the goal having fun
while you play?  How much do those two interact?  But role
playing tends to interfere with maximizing the chance of ascending.
If you haven't ascended in a few years it might not be the most fun
way to approach the game.

> Some people will
> (rightly) say that your initial weapon is worthless and sometimes it's
> counter-productive in the long run as you might be developing a "wrong"
> weapon class that has no good advanced weapons.

That greatly depends on the character class.  Valkyrie starts with
a long sword.  Since ascendable artifacts Excalibur and Frost
Brand are long swords it does make good sense for that class.
Wizard starts with quarterstaff and while it's okay as a starting
weapon burning the skill slots to bring it up to max means not
being able to enhance stuff that's more improtant later in the game.

> Granted I'm not
> experienced (the deepest I've been is level 18 in one lucky game),

Maybe role playing isn't your best plan yet.

> I don't have a good idea of advanced weapons.

As players progress in the game there is lesson after lesson to
learn and skill after skill to acquire.  How to retreat and survive,
inventory management to not be burdened except when a level
is cleared out to shuttle your stash to the down stairs, remote
combat, skill slot management, building your carried ascension
kit, building the stash of items for the planes, and so on.

> So here's my question: do
> you develop your starting weapon classes and why? Or do you dump them
> for a better weapon the first chance you get? Examples that come to mind
> is Healer's scalpel or archeologists bull whip... What do you think? You
> can also talk about other weapons/classes/examples without spoiling me
> too much. :)

The depends on the class.  When I first start playing a class I
try a few games and see how it goes.  Then I take a step back
and examine their max skill levels in the various weapons classes,
spells classes and what artifacts they can expect to get.  Once
I know those possibilities I develop a chart of how many skill
slots it costs to reach each intermediate skill level in each option.
Since getting an artifact gift unrestricts a skill slot I only consider
a few of those options - the "good" artifacts.  Then I start trimming
out the ones that would be expensive for little gain.

Having a table of what costs how many skills slots I come up with
some options for a tuned killing machine at XP 30, then several
alternate orderings to get from the starting point to there.  Then
as I level up each time I have the option of enhancing a weapons
class I compare it to the end point.  If it's on that path I do it.

If it's off that path I consider that current game.  Will enhancing
a slot that won't be needed in the late game really make a big
difference in survivability in my current situation?  If it will then I
do it and take the late-game cost.

Note that luck overcomes a *lot* of skill settings.  With maxxed
out luck it's easy to get into a situation where an restricted
weapons class never seems to miss.  Since luck can be maxed
out at the first post-mines-end altar, worries about enhancing
a weapons class off the long term path only tend to come up
beofre finishing the mines and Sokoban.



